

I don't want to have to fuck around with determining what line we're on, or skipping lines when drawing from the global.main_txt_ar. I simply want all of the text in the global.main_txt_ar to be formatted so that if it exceeds our dimension variables, it's broken up and the remainder of the text is added to the next index in the array.
	So now when we draw our global.main_txt_ar, it's as simple as iterating through each line in the array.

scr_parse_str_for_main_txt_ar:

We create a temp_str_ar, an empty array. cur_txt = “”, ar_ind = 0, cur_char_ind = 0, char_count_int = 0. 

We iterate through each character in our string, adding them to our cur_text string variable as we do. 
	If our char_count exceeds our global.main_txt_max_str_w and the current_char is not a space, then we start iterating backwards, decrementing cur_char_ind  and removing each char from our cur_text variable as we do so, until we encounter a space character, at which point we add our cur_txt to our temp_str_ar, increment ar_ind, reset cur_txt = “” and char_count_int = 0, and continue in our main loop.

	Else if char_count exceeds our global.main_txt_max_str_w and the current_char IS a space, then we simply add the cur_text to our temp_str_ar, advance everything as normal.
